# Security headers for platform-served static assets.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The Flask app set these in an after_request hook, on
# every response, because "a policy applied to some responses is a policy with
# a hole in it" (contex/web/security.py). Here static assets are served by the
# platform without invoking the Worker -- that is what keeps them free and
# unmetered -- so the Worker cannot add headers to them. This file does.
#
# The Worker applies the same set to its own API responses, so the two paths
# together reproduce the original's coverage.
#
# NO NONCE, and that is an improvement rather than a loss. The Flask templates
# carried inline <script> blocks, so the policy needed a per-request nonce to
# avoid 'unsafe-inline'. This frontend has no inline script at all -- app.js is
# an external module -- so 'self' alone is strictly stronger: there is nothing
# for a nonce to authorise, and nothing an injected nonce could forge.
#
# Deliberately absent: COOP/COEP. Cross-origin isolation would buy
# SharedArrayBuffer for multi-threaded ONNX and would break the Firebase Auth
# popup and the gstatic SDK load. See spec R3.

# THE FIREBASE ORIGINS are the same three security.py names, and for the same
# reasons: gstatic serves the browser SDK, apis.google.com serves the sign-in
# iframe, and the project's own authDomain is where the popup completes.
# Flask read the authDomain from config and put it in the policy per request;
# a static file cannot, so the checked-in default names the pattern instead --
# a host pattern is a wider surface than a host, and there is no reason to keep
# it wider than one domain once the project is fixed. It has been narrowed:
# `npm run build:headers contex-28bfd.firebaseapp.com` wrote the exact domain
# below, and running it with no argument restores the pattern.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' https://www.gstatic.com https://apis.google.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; style-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.googleusercontent.com; media-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self' blob: https://www.gstatic.com https://apis.google.com https://contex-28bfd.firebaseapp.com; connect-src 'self' https://identitytoolkit.googleapis.com https://securetoken.googleapis.com https://www.googleapis.com https://contex-28bfd.firebaseapp.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

# The engine, its 9.88 MB format file and the TeX Live tree are content-addressed
# by build: they never change under a given deploy. Without this the format is
# refetched on every compile, which on a phone is the difference between a
# preview and a stall. Static assets are free and unmetered, so caching them
# hard costs nothing and saves the user the bytes.
/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/texmf/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# AND THE PATH THE ENGINE ACTUALLY ASKS FOR. The rule above never applied to a
# single engine fetch. kpathsea requests /pdftex/<format>/<name>, which
# _redirects rewrites to /texmf/<name> -- but these rules match the REQUEST
# path, not the rewritten one, so every one of those responses went out with no
# Cache-Control at all. The 9.88 MB format file was re-downloaded on every
# visit: 1 s on a desk, ~50 s on a phone at 1.6 Mbps, against a 60 s engine
# load timeout. That is the stall the comment above was written to prevent.
#
# The tree is content-addressed by build, so the same reasoning applies
# verbatim here; it is the same bytes under a different address.
/pdftex/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# NOT COMPRESSED, AND MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The format file is 95% of
# a first visit: 10.36 MB of the ~10.9 MB fetched before anything can compile,
# and it gzips to 2.03 MB. Everything else here arrives compressed -- the .wasm
# is brotli'd -- but `.fmt` is an extension the edge does not recognise, so it
# is served with no Content-Type and no compression.
#
# Declaring `Content-Type: application/octet-stream` was tried and does not
# help: the header applies (verified on /texmf/swiftlatexpdftex.fmt) but the
# edge does not compress that type. The two things that would work are both
# refused here:
#
#   - calling it a text type, which is a lie about a binary;
#   - shipping it pre-gzipped with Content-Encoding, which has to name the
#     engine's own address to avoid breaking every other file under /pdftex/.
#     That address is /pdftex/<format>/<name>, and a middle wildcard such as
#     `/pdftex/*/swiftlatexpdftex.fmt` does NOT match here (also verified), so
#     it would have to hard-code the format number -- and if that number ever
#     changed the browser would hand the engine gzip bytes labelled as plain
#     ones, which fails as a corrupt format file rather than as a 404.
#
# So the first compile stays expensive and the cache above makes it once-only.

# The offline recognisers: 61 MB of ONNX Runtime, pix2text-mfr and Tesseract's
# language data, all fixed by build. They are fetched only when someone accepts
# the offline conversion, and refetching them on the next one would make that
# path unusable on a phone. Static assets are free and unmetered, so caching
# them for a year costs nothing and saves the user the bytes.
/models/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The two typefaces, ten subset files, fixed by build. Served from here so a
# page load contacts nobody but Cloudflare - see the Privacy Policy. If one is
# ever replaced, give it a new name: this rule tells the browser never to ask
# again.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
